is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router0
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The FRR/RIPv2 routing daemon configuration on router0. The running configuration is completely empty — no `router rip` block, no `network` statements, no routing protocol of any kind.

**Mechanism:** The topology relies on RIPv2 for inter-segment route exchange. Router1 is correctly running RIPv2 on all four of its networks, including the `10.0.0.0/8` WAN segment shared with router0 (`wan_r0_r1`). Because router0 has no RIP configured, it never participates in the RIP exchange. Two cascading failures result: (1) router0 never learns routes to `192.168.3.0/24` (Office) or `192.168.4.0/24` (Principle) from router1, so return traffic from CS/IT hosts is black-holed at router0; (2) router1 never receives RIP updates from router0 advertising `192.168.1.0/24` (IT) and `192.168.2.0/24` (CS), so any initial stale entries router1 may have held eventually expired past the 180-second RIP timeout — confirmed by office_pc receiving "Destination Net Unreachable" directly from router1 (192.168.3.1) on every probe.

**Localization:** router0, FRR process, all interfaces (eth0/eth1/eth2). The WAN interfaces on router1 (eth2 on `wan_r0_r1`) are physically UP with zero errors and zero drops — the user's hypothesis about a router1 WAN uplink fault is incorrect.

**Root cause:** Router0's FRR configuration is missing a `router rip` section entirely; it has never joined the RIP domain.

**Fix:** Add to router0's `/etc/frr/frr.conf`: `router rip` with `network 10.0.0.0/8`, `network 192.168.1.0/24`, `network 192.168.2.0/24`, and `version 2`, then reload FRR (`service frr reload`). This will re-establish bidirectional RIP exchange on the `wan_r0_r1` link and propagate missing routes to both peers.
=== END ===